1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,424
Thursday, six in the evening.

2
00:00:02,474 --> 00:00:06,600
A buyer orders roses from Malika's shop and presses Pay.

3
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:08,904
The buyer's bank says yes.

4
00:00:08,954 --> 00:00:16,056
Then the line to the card processing centre goes quiet for forty seconds, and the yes never arrives.

5
00:00:16,106 --> 00:00:20,480
What should the page tell the buyer now?

6
00:00:20,530 --> 00:00:26,216
Timur runs the bank's card zone, and he has a rule for exactly this moment.

7
00:00:26,266 --> 00:00:31,952
In Lesson 7, the card zone checked who was calling and agreed to charge the card.

8
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:36,368
This lesson follows that charge out of the card zone, to the centre.

9
00:00:36,418 --> 00:00:44,264
Behind every card network sits a card processing centre: the switchboard that carries a payment to the buyer's bank.

10
00:00:44,314 --> 00:00:46,472
Each centre has its own protocol.

11
00:00:46,522 --> 00:00:52,688
Its own field names, its own long list of error codes, and its own way of saying yes.

12
00:00:52,738 --> 00:01:01,400
If the card zone spoke every centre's language itself, every new centre would mean changing the code that guards card numbers.

13
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:05,624
Timur wants that code to change as rarely as possible.

14
00:01:05,674 --> 00:01:08,744
So the card zone has one adapter per centre.

15
00:01:08,794 --> 00:01:12,224
An adapter is a small, separate service.

16
00:01:12,274 --> 00:01:15,800
On one side, it speaks the centre's language.

17
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:21,272
On the other side, it speaks one plain language that the card zone understands.

18
00:01:21,322 --> 00:01:25,016
Every adapter takes the same three requests.

19
00:01:25,066 --> 00:01:26,888
Charge a card.

20
00:01:26,938 --> 00:01:28,760
Give money back.

21
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:30,656
Ask what happened to a payment.

22
00:01:30,706 --> 00:01:35,048
The card zone never needs to know which centre is behind the adapter.

23
00:01:35,098 --> 00:01:37,328
The answers come back the same way.

24
00:01:37,378 --> 00:01:45,440
Each centre says yes and no in hundreds of ways, and the adapter turns every answer into one of three outcomes.

25
00:01:45,490 --> 00:01:47,840
Approved means the money moved.

26
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:52,160
Declined means the buyer's bank said no, and no money moved.

27
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:55,616
Unknown means the system does not know yet.

28
00:01:55,666 --> 00:01:57,920
A timeout is unknown.

29
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:01,688
A broken or unreadable answer is unknown.

30
00:02:01,738 --> 00:02:07,840
So when the line goes silent, is that a yes or a no?

31
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:14,872
Here is Timur's rule, and I agree with him completely: unknown is never declined.

32
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:19,456
Most teams get this wrong, because no is simpler to handle than maybe.

33
00:02:19,506 --> 00:02:28,144
On Thursday evening, reading silence as no would have told the buyer the payment failed, while their bank had already said yes.

34
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:31,408
The buyer pays again, and pays twice.

35
00:02:31,458 --> 00:02:38,920
So every charge carries the system's own payment id, and the centre keeps that id with the payment.

36
00:02:38,970 --> 00:02:44,176
When the line comes back, the card zone asks the centre about exactly that payment.

37
00:02:44,226 --> 00:02:46,192
Asking never moves money.

38
00:02:46,242 --> 00:02:48,064
It only reads what happened.

39
00:02:48,114 --> 00:02:50,344
This is what Malika's buyer saw.

40
00:02:50,394 --> 00:02:56,248
After pressing Pay, the page shows a spinner and the words "Taking the payment".

41
00:02:56,298 --> 00:03:02,176
A short line says the buyer's bank is being asked, and that this takes a few seconds.

42
00:03:02,226 --> 00:03:08,896
Behind that line, the card zone hands the charge to an adapter, and the adapter talks to its centre.

43
00:03:08,946 --> 00:03:11,848
On most evenings, a yes comes back.

44
00:03:11,898 --> 00:03:16,528
The page turns to "Paid" and shows a reference number from the network.

45
00:03:16,578 --> 00:03:28,216
When the bank says no, the page shows "Payment declined" with a plain reason, such as "There isn't enough money on the card." But on Thursday, the centre went silent.

46
00:03:28,266 --> 00:03:30,472
The page did not say declined.

47
00:03:30,522 --> 00:03:35,368
It said "We're confirming your payment", and told the buyer not to pay again.

48
00:03:35,418 --> 00:03:42,352
Within minutes, the system asked the centre about that one payment id, and the page turned to Paid.

49
00:03:42,402 --> 00:03:44,752
Timur, meanwhile, got an alert.

50
00:03:44,802 --> 00:03:49,576
It named the adapter that could not reach its centre in the last few minutes.

51
00:03:49,626 --> 00:03:53,896
It warned that charges through that adapter fail or stay unknown.

52
00:03:53,946 --> 00:03:57,688
Next year, the bank joins a new card network.

53
00:03:57,738 --> 00:04:02,992
Timur adds one adapter, and nothing else in the card zone changes.

54
00:04:03,042 --> 00:04:07,960
And on that Thursday, one buyer paid for roses exactly once.

55
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:14,344
To recap: the adapter turns every answer into approved, declined or unknown.

56
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:19,296
When the centre goes silent, what is the answer?

57
00:04:19,346 --> 00:04:21,480
Unknown, never declined.

58
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:25,440
Next: how the card zone keeps a card number safe.
